Let $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For analytic functions $f, g : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C}$, we say $f$ is subordinate to $g$, written $f \prec g$, if there exists an analytic function $\omega : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{D}$ with $\omega(0) = 0$ such that $f(z) = g(\omega(z))$ for all $z \in \mathbb{D}$. Given an analytic function
$$g(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} B_n z^n, \qquad z \in \mathbb{D},$$
let $S_g = \{ f : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C} \text{ analytic} : f \prec g\}$, and let $\overline{\mathrm{co}}(S_g)$ denote the closed convex hull of $S_g$ in the topology of locally uniform convergence on $\mathbb{D}$. The rotations $g_t(z) := g(z e^{it})$, $0 \le t < 2\pi$, always belong to $S_g$ and are extreme points of $\overline{\mathrm{co}}(S_g)$ in known examples such as
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $g(z) = \left(\frac{1+cz}{1-z}\right)^{\alpha}$ with $|c| \le 1$ and $\alpha \ge 1$;
\item[(b)] $g(z) = \exp\!\left(\frac{1+z}{1-z}\right)$;
\item[(c)] $g(z) = h\!\left(\frac{1+z}{1-z}\right)$, where $h$ is a univalent quadratic polynomial on the half-plane $\{w : \mathrm{Re}\, w > 0\}$.
\end{itemize}

Determine general conditions on $g$ which ensure that the only extreme points of $\overline{\mathrm{co}}(S_g)$ are the rotations $\{g(z e^{it}) : 0 \le t < 2\pi\}$. In particular, does this conclusion hold whenever the Taylor coefficients $B_n$ of $g$ are positive and increasing in a suitably regular manner?

