A symmetry-protected topological (SPT) phase in (d+1) spacetime dimensions is a gapped phase of matter with a global symmetry G that is short-range entangled in the bulk and whose nontriviality is detected by anomalous boundary degrees of freedom of dimension d. In (2+1) dimensions, boundary conformal field theory (BCFT) techniques have been developed to diagnose and characterize SPT phases: by placing a (1+1)-dimensional CFT on a half-space or interval and classifying the conformal boundary conditions (together with their associated boundary states, g-functions, and anomaly inflow data), one can detect the bulk SPT class and its symmetry anomalies.

Formulate and develop a higher-dimensional analogue of this program. Specifically, construct a framework of boundary/defect conformal field theory in d ≥ 3 spacetime dimensions in which:

(i) one defines the appropriate notion of conformal boundary conditions for a (3+1)-dimensional bulk theory whose boundary supports a (2+1)-dimensional surface theory;
(ii) the framework accommodates surface theories that are either gapless conformal field theories or that carry intrinsic topological order (i.e. are described by topological quantum field theories with anyonic excitations);
(iii) the boundary/defect data (boundary central charges, anomaly coefficients, defect operator content, etc.) provide invariants that distinguish distinct (3+1)-dimensional SPT phases protected by a given symmetry group G, including time-reversal and unitary internal symmetries.

Prove or disprove the existence of such a framework, and if it exists, give explicit invariants and computations characterizing nontrivial (3+1)-dimensional bosonic and fermionic SPT phases via these higher-dimensional BCFT methods.

