A quasi Riemann surface is a structure generalizing the notion of a Riemann surface in which one is given a collection of "quasi holomorphic curves" each carrying a one-complex-dimensional analytic structure, together with compatibility data on overlaps that allow one to formulate a Cauchy–Riemann-type equation globally. On an ordinary Riemann surface, complex analysis provides: holomorphic and meromorphic functions and differentials, the Cauchy–Riemann equations, contour integration and the residue theorem, the Riemann–Roch theorem, period matrices, theta functions, uniformization, and moduli of complex structures.

Develop the analogous theory of complex analysis on a quasi Riemann surface. Specifically, formulate and establish on a quasi Riemann surface the natural analogues of:
(a) holomorphic and meromorphic functions and differential forms;
(b) the Cauchy–Riemann equations and Cauchy-type integral formulas;
(c) a residue calculus and period structure;
(d) a Riemann–Roch-type theorem relating dimensions of spaces of meromorphic sections to a topological/analytic invariant;
(e) moduli of quasi complex structures and an analogue of uniformization.

Provide definitions and proofs that parallel the classical theory on genuine Riemann surfaces, and identify the precise sense in which classical complex analysis is recovered when the quasi Riemann surface reduces to an ordinary Riemann surface.

